    "natural_summary": "<div class='jmv-clinical-summary'><h3> Plain Language Summary<\/h3><p><strong>Study Design:<\/strong> Non-inferiority Trial for Overall Survival<\/p><p><strong>Sample Size Calculation:<\/strong> To detect a hazard ratio of 1 (0% higher hazard) with 80% power at   2.5% significance level.<\/p><p><strong>Clinical Impact:<\/strong> No assumed difference between arms. Whether this is clinically worthwhile depends on the absolute survival gain and the disease setting.<\/p><p><strong>Study Timeline:<\/strong> 24 months recruitment + 12 months follow-up = 36 months total.<\/p><div class='references' style='margin-top: 15px; padding-top: 10px; border-top: 1px solid #ddd; font-size: 0.9em;'><p><strong>Methods:<\/strong> Sample size calculations based on non-inferiority margin approach (Rothmann et al. 2003). Exponential survival distribution assumed with uniform accrual pattern.<\/p><\/div><\/div>",
    "interpretation": "<p><strong>Power Analysis for Survival Study<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Study Design:<\/strong><br>• Analysis Type: Calculate Sample Size<br>• Statistical Test: Non-inferiority Trial<br>• Design: Two-arm Parallel<br>• Primary Endpoint: Overall Survival<\/p><p><strong>Statistical Parameters:<\/strong><br>• Hazard Ratio: 1<br>• Significance Level (alpha): 0.025 (2.5%)<br>• Statistical Power: 0.8 (80%)<br>• Allocation Ratio: 1:1<br><\/p><p><strong>Population Characteristics:<\/strong><br>• Control Median Survival: 12 months<br>• Expected Treatment Median: 12 months<br>• Accrual Period: 24 months<br>• Follow-up Period: 12 months<br>• Annual Dropout Rate: 5%<\/p><p><strong>Objective:<\/strong><br>Determine the required sample size to detect a hazard ratio of 1 with 80% power at a   2.5% significance level.<\/p><p><strong>Clinical Interpretation:<\/strong><\/p><p><strong>Sample Size Interpretation:<\/strong><br>To detect a hazard ratio of 1 with 80% power and   2.5% significance level, you need approximately 901 total subjects.<\/p>A Non-inferiority Trial power analysis was conducted to determine the sample size required to rule out a hazard ratio of 1.25 or worse, assuming a true hazard ratio of 1.00 with 80% power at a   2.5% significance level (one-sided). The analysis indicates that 901 subjects are needed, with an accrual period of 24 months and additional follow-up of 12 months."
